text:
tanya: Fresh run of the Next starter uncovered three onboarding issues I wanted to flag: env var naming exposes secrets, Python LlamaIndex example still uses generate(), and our eval CI is flaky because of streaming. Short repros and suggested fixes below.

liam: can you paste the minimal repro for the 401?

tanya: yep. clone repo, cp .env.example .env, npm run dev, call /api/chat. Server code reads process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY but .env.example only has NEXT_PUBLIC_REDWOOD_API_KEY. Result is 401 on server because the secret never loads. snippet: 
```js
// pages/api/chat.js
export default async function handler(req, res) {
  const key = process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY; // undefined for users who copied NEXT_PUBLIC_ var
  const r = await fetch('https://api.redwood.ai/v1/responses', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${key}` },
    body: JSON.stringify({ input: req.body.prompt })
  });
  res.status(r.status).json(await r.json());
}
```

nora: oof. thats a confusing starter state for new users. we should make REDWOOD_API_KEY canonical in .env.example and add an explicit comment that NEXT_PUBLIC_* is client-exposed.

kevin: also ran the python adapter samples: LlamaIndex example imports agent.generate(...) which is the old surface. New SDK uses responses.create. We should update official Python examples and provide a tiny compat shim so existing user forks dont break. quick shim idea: 
```py
# samples/compat/responses_compat.py
from redwood import responses

def create_compat(*args, **kwargs):
    if hasattr(responses, 'create'):
        return responses.create(*args, **kwargs)
    return responses.generate(*args, **kwargs)
```

liam: good. That reduces churn and keeps samples working across SDK pins. also add a note in sample README about which SDK versions map to which method names.

ci-bot: test run #892 failed: harness_snapshot_test (timeout)

kevin: about the eval flakes: some models stream tokens by default; the harness currently reads as they arrive and some snapshot tests expect full-string outputs. Proposed quick fixes: add --no-stream (or --capture final) for CI runs so snapshots are deterministic, and add one retry on transient network / model timeouts.

tanya: +1. also recommend adding a small withRetries util in the JS sample but explicitly do NOT retry on 401 unless the flow detects token rotation. Example util: 
```ts
// samples/utils/retry.ts
export async function withRetries(fn, retries = 2) {
  let i = 0;
  while (true) {
    try { return await fn(); }
    catch (e) {
      if (e?.status === 401 || i++ >= retries) throw e;
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 250 * i));
    }
  }
}
```

liam: plan of action: 1) update .env.example -> REDWOOD_API_KEY with server-only comment; 2) add retry util + docs caveat about not masking 401; 3) add python compat shim and bump sample readmes; 4) add --capture-mode to eval harness and default CI to "final". I'll open a PR bundling these.

nora: will add a short "first-run checklist" to the repo README (env var, node version, how to run harness with --capture-mode final). saves new users time.

ci-bot: opened issue #472: "next-starter: env var mismatch, python compat, eval streaming flakiness" assigned to @liam.

kevin: i'll draft the eval runner changes and a small test that fails if streaming is enabled in CI mode.

liam: PR opened: https://github.com/redwood-inference/samples/pull/641  includes fixes and notes. pinging for reviews.

tanya: thanks team! small docs + tiny compat helpers go a long way for onboarding.
